  "articleBody": "Replacing a kitchen sink and faucet seems straightforward until you discover old valve corrosion, mismatched countertop holes, or cramped supply lines. Before you buy new fixtures or schedule installation, review these key points to avoid surprises.\n\nWhat to Prepare Before You Start\n- Count your faucet mounting holes: Check how many holes are drilled into your countertop or sink deck. A single-handle faucet requires a deck plate if you are replacing an older three-hole setup.\n- Measure basin depth and faucet reach: High-arc spouts offer great pot clearance, but pairing one with a shallow sink can cause splashing onto your backsplash.\n- Inspect your shutoff valves and supply lines: Look under the cabinet for signs of corrosion or moisture around the angle stops. Reusing brittle, older valves often leads to slow leaks under pressure.\n- Check drain pipe alignment: If your new sink is deeper than the old one, your P-trap and waste arm might sit too low, requiring drain pipe reconfiguration.\n\nWhen Professional Guidance Helps\nIf your shutoff valves do not turn off completely, your drain lines show mineral buildup, or you are mounting an undermount sink to stone counters, having a professional handle the job protects your cabinetry from costly water damage.\n\nFor clean, reliable sink and faucet installations throughout the greater Denver area, call HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to book your project.",
  "datePublished": "2026-10-23"
}